data.content:
The arguments are indeed swapped. `Assert.AreEqual` expects **(expected, actual)**, but your code passes `(result, 5)` — putting the actual value first. This causes misleading failure messages like *"Expected: <result> but was: <5>"*.

**Fix:** swap the arguments so the literal expected value comes first:

```csharp
// Before
Assert.AreEqual(result, 5);
Assert.AreEqual(result, 0);

// After
Assert.AreEqual(5, result);
Assert.AreEqual(0, result);
```

This applies to all MSTest `Assert.AreEqual` / `AreNotEqual` calls — the convention is always `(expected, actual[, message])`.
